1px;\"><div><mark class=\"trash\"><i>𝒹𝓇𝑒𝒶𝓂𝓈 𝒹𝑜𝓃’𝓉 𝓌𝑜𝓇𝓀 𝓊𝓃𝓁𝑒𝓈𝓈 𝓎𝑜𝓊 𝒹𝑜</i></mark></div></div><div class=\"text-snippet\" style=\"top: 357px; left: 913px; font-size: 17px; max-width: 368px;\"><div><mark class=\"trash\"><i>: ¨ ·.· ¨ : ` · .. · `</i></mark></div></div><div class=\"text-snippet\" style=\"top: 284px; left: 977.5px; font-size: 21px; max-width: 325px;\"><div><mark class=\"link\"><a target=\"_blank\" href=\"https://www.nytimes.com/2021/03/23/magazine/addison-rae-beauty-industry.html\">Yet it was hard to s</a></mark></div></div><div style=\"width: 673px; font-size: 34px; visibility: hidden;\">*Boyfriend hides phone because it keeps making me upset and he says I should stop doing this project*</div><div style=\"width: 712px; font-size: 34px; visibility: hidden;\">It’s not that I want company, but that I want to be affirmed by another pair of eyes.</div><div style=\"width: 761px; font-size: 36px; visibility: hidden;\">𝒹𝓇𝑒𝒶𝓂𝓈 𝒹𝑜𝓃’𝓉 𝓌𝑜𝓇𝓀 𝓊𝓃𝓁𝑒𝓈𝓈 𝓎𝑜𝓊 𝒹𝑜</div><div style=\"width: 368px; font-size: 17px; visibility: hidden;\">: ¨ ·.· ¨ : ` · .. · `</div><div style=\"width: 325px; font-size: 21px; visibility: hidden;\">Yet it was hard to shake the feeling that the industry seems to be counting on everyone’s agreeing that beauty culture is solely empowering, obscuring a dialectic of confidence-building and -destroying, kept afloat by hyperreal images on our phones.</div></div></body></html>"
